          { name: "Alaska Native Population Absence from Partisan Result", tag: "careful", why: "The 96% unopposed result tells us nothing about how the ~16.77% Alaska Native population actually evaluates the incumbent, because they had no organized alternative to express preference. Running unopposed is a luxury that can conceal genuine grievance or simple absence of capacity to mount opposition. The strategic question is whether Alaska Native constituents feel represented or simply unrepresented; an opponent who activates this population around subsistence, energy, or healthcare could materially erode the current margin." },
          { name: "Treating 96% Unopposed as a Permanent Supermajority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 96% result achieved without opposition is a baseline that reflects absence of organized alternative, not a stable supermajority cushion. In Alaska, Alaska Native constituencies have demonstrated the capacity to organize when they perceive governing indifference or when a credible alternative emerges. An incumbent who campaigns this district as though 96% is a permanent floor — and who neglects proactive Alaska Native engagement — is arithmetically overconfident about a margin that has never been tested in genuine competition." },
          { name: "Positioning Alaska Native Concerns as a Special Interest Rather Than a Governing Priority", tag: "avoid", why: "In Alaska districts where Alaska Native population approaches or exceeds 15%, framing subsistence rights, energy policy, or healthcare access as special-interest accommodation rather than core governance obligation actively alienates the constituency and invites organized opposition. The ~16.77% gap represents constituents with genuine material stakes in subsistence, energy development, and rural healthcare access. A representative whose rhetoric or record treats these as add-ons rather than primary governing concerns signals to Alaska Native constituents that they are peripheral to the incumbent's coalition, which is both a governance failure and an electoral vulnerability." }
