          { name: "Pandering to Constituencies as Opposed to Serving Them", tag: "avoid", why: "AK-SD-G's college-educated, professional, and immigrant constituencies have sophisticated policy expectations and recognize performative engagement; any candidate who offers symbolic acknowledgment rather than concrete legislative action on education, professional opportunity, housing affordability, or immigration integration will lose credibility with these voters. Similarly, token appearances in Asian or renter community spaces without real policy commitments will be perceived as opportunistic rather than authentic. These constituencies have options and will vote against a candidate who patronizes them." },
          { name: "Allowing Alaska Native Constituent Needs to Become Invisible", tag: "avoid", why: "The ~14% demographic gap almost certainly represents Alaska Native residents whose healthcare access, economic development, tribal sovereignty, and cultural concerns are distinct from the broader professional constituency — and whose civic participation can shift outcomes if they feel neglected by the incumbent. In a district where the dominant electoral story is college-educated professionals and Asian immigrants, Alaska Native communities can become invisible in constituent service and resource allocation. A representative who fails to engage this constituency specifically — not as an afterthought to the broader minority narrative — risks alienating voters capable of determining the outcome in a 56-43 seat." }
