          "Monitor high-income defection risk: the 44% opposition in a district this affluent is not a floor—it reflects persuadable voters who may view Democratic governance as competent but not optimal for their economic interests; any erosion in school quality perception, perceived fiscal mismanagement, or rising tax burden on professional households is sufficient to shift this seat materially.",
          "Engage the Alaska Native ~18% gap with specificity: while the professional-class majority sets the partisan tone, the Alaska Native community represents a meaningful constituency with distinct interests in subsistence rights, economic development, and healthcare access; treating this community as peripheral rather than constituent is both a governance failure and a latent electoral vulnerability.",
          "Treat renter vs. homeowner economic divergence as a latent internal issue: at 26.5% renters, the district is homeowner-dominant, but younger renters in early wealth accumulation are a different political constituency than established homeowners; housing cost pressure, student debt, and economic mobility are the material concerns for renters that differ from the property-value-focused interests of the 73.5% homeowner majority."
